# AI Agent Directives — Generated GraphQL Client

> **NOTICE FOR AI AGENTS AND DEVELOPERS**:
> This directory contains auto-generated build artifacts produced by Salep.
> Do not manually edit, format, rename, or delete files in this directory. Regeneration overwrites them.

## How to change generated code

1. Edit the GraphQL schema file.
2. Edit or add `.graphql` operation documents.
3. Change generator configuration for namespaces, client naming, scalar mappings, union representation, and transport options.
4. Rebuild to regenerate the client.

## How to consume the client

- Treat generated files as a read-only, strongly typed API. Inspect operation and response models to discover methods and fields.
- HTTP GET for query operations is {{ if settings.use_http_get # begin if }}enabled{{ else # else }}disabled{{ end # end if }}. Batching is {{ if settings.enable_batching # begin if }}enabled{{ else # else }}disabled{{ end # end if }}.
{{~ if csharp.use_native_unions # begin if ~}}
- GraphQL unions and interfaces use native C# union declarations. Handle them with exhaustive type-pattern `switch` expressions.
{{~ else # else ~}}
- GraphQL unions and interfaces use Dunet discriminated unions. Use exhaustive `.Match(...)` methods; do not cast or use type-pattern checks.
{{~ end # end if ~}}
- Register `{{ settings.client_name }}` with `IHttpClientFactory` and provide the GraphQL endpoint URI.
- Check `response.Errors` before relying on `response.Data`. GraphQL can return partial data together with errors.
- For multi-project generation, build referenced client projects first. Their manifests define ownership of shared generated types; downstream generation suppresses ancestor-owned declarations.
